WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:27.560
"A Quiet Place" was one of the most
unlikely hits of 2018.

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:39.059
Made on a budget of just 17 million
dollars the horror film would go on to

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:45.539
gross more than 332 million dollars
worldwide. Emily Blunt earned praise for

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.010
her performance and actor John Krasinski
had his first major breakthrough as a

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:58.049
director. One thing that makes the movie
so unique: its use of sound. "A Quiet Place"

00:00:58.049 --> 00:01:03.000
is set in a post-apocalyptic world
overrun with monsters with ultra

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.030
sensitive hearing. Make a single sound
and you're in big trouble

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:13.110
therefore the characters communicate in
sign language and make the lightest of

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:22.590
footsteps. While the movie has a
groundbreaking lack of sound that

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.929
doesn't mean it's completely absent of
it. Brandon Jones, Ethan van Der Ryn and

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:33.209
Eric Aadahl of E Squared are responsible
for much of what you hear on screen.

00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:38.640
"Sound was actually written into the
script and so for us it was like a dream

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.049
come true like kids in a candy store."
Here's how some of the movie's most

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:49.020
notable sounds were made. Foley, a method
in which objects are used to create

00:01:49.020 --> 00:02:00.240
sounds made by humans, or in this case
monsters, was used. "A Foley stage is a

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.210
super quiet environment where we can put
picture up on the screen and then record

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:14.280
using our microphones in sync with the
picture." Some sounds like a shotgun

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:21.560
loading and water splashing were made
exactly as you'd expect.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.880
For other sounds they had to get
creative

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:59.840
The most unusual sound might be the
echolocation sounds used to illustrate

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.510
how the monsters get around without
vision. To make this sound they settled

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.239
on putting a stun gun up to a patch of
grapes.

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:17.030
They then manipulated the sound in
the next phase: the editing process. "The

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.200
whole concept with these creatures is
there's kind of an electromagnetic

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.340
component so using something electrical
as a source for the vocals seemed to kind

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:34.250
of make sense. So here's our recording of
the stun gun [buzzing] We can take that sound and

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.420
slow it down
and we can hear the individual electric

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.750
clicks

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:53.819
Not every sound effect requires Foley
for the sound of a baby crying Brandon

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:58.560
Jones recorded the sound of his baby
nephew crying. One of the biggest

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.819
challenges of this movie wasn't what you
heard with ease, but the kind of sounds

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:07.800
we might normally tune out. Footsteps are
a classic Foley noise any sound

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:19.919
designer is used to making. Usually it'll
be loud... but for "A Quiet Place" the team

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:23.690
had to account for the fact that the
characters mostly walked around barefoot.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:29.220
"Where we would play Foley footsteps,
and where not to, were really dictated by the

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:35.040
logic of the film. On a lot of why shots
we didn't even shoot the Foley for those

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.650
feet if we can hear it from such a
distance

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:42.180
the creatures can too. And then when you
cut into an extreme close-up and you see

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:48.479
a foot push through sand then we hear it
just chiaroscuro." The scariest sound of

00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:53.610
all though: the lack of sound! "It's rare
that you get to work on a movie that has

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:58.979
this much like absolute silence and
parts where music and dialogue's all

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:03.810
stripped away and it's just these faint
details. One of the biggest things that

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.320
happened throughout the course of
post-production for this movie was

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:11.729
really scaling back and being more
minimalist in the creature sound design

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:16.770
because our first pass had him making
noise all over the place and we kept

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.720
scaling back and making it kind of a 'less
is more' approach." Following in the path

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.120
of one of the great monster movies
before it "Jaws," the less we see, or in

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.710
this case the less we hear from the
monsters, the more terrifying they are

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:38.120
and yet good luck getting that stun gun
sound out of your head now

